import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import ArchitectureDiagram from '../../components/architecture/ArchitectureDiagram';

// Authored as a standalone Starlight page (not a content-collection entry) so the
// diagram can be a real Astro island: `starlight-llms-txt` renders every docs
// content entry through a container with no React renderer and would crash the
// /llms-full.txt build on an embedded framework component. A src/pages route is
// outside that collection, so the island renders normally in dev and build.
---

<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title: 'Architecture Overview',
    description:
      'How Maelstrom works: the feedback loop between the browser and a server-side LLM, and the one contract they speak.',
    // A src/pages route is not a content-collection entry, so Starlight cannot
    // auto-derive these the way it does for sibling docs. Set them explicitly:
    // the GitHub edit link, and prev/next pagination to the neighbouring
    // Understanding pages (matching the sidebar order).
    editUrl:
      'https://github.com/maelstrom-co/maelstrom/edit/main/apps/docs/src/pages/understanding/architecture.astro',
    prev: { link: '/understanding/comparison/', label: 'How Maelstrom compares' },
    next: {
      link: '/understanding/voice-architecture/',
      label: 'The voice boundary',
    },
  }}
>
  <p>
    Maelstrom turns a user's intent into a laid-out UI by running a loop between
    your browser and an LLM on the server. Browser packages describe the current
    state and capture input, the server <code>engine</code> decides what to show,
    and <code>protocol</code> is the single contract both sides speak. The diagram
    below traces one trip around that loop — press play, or explore a package.
  </p>

  <ArchitectureDiagram client:load />

  <h2>The round-trip, in words</h2>

  <h3>It starts with a reason</h3>
  <p>
    Every update begins with an <code>UpdateReason</code>, and there are three: a
    <code>user_intent</code> (someone asks for something), a
    <code>chart_resize</code> (the available grid changed), or a
    <code>vessel_mutation</code> (a component changed its own state).
    <a href="/understanding/llm-orchestration/"><code>@maelstrom-co/client</code></a>
    batches the pending reasons and builds a <code>ChartRequest</code> — a snapshot
    of the current Vessel instances, the chart's dimensions <em>in grid cells</em>,
    the registered Vessels, and the layout mode — then sends it across the
    deployment boundary.
  </p>

  <h3>The engine decides</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="/understanding/engine/"><code>@maelstrom-co/engine</code></a> holds
    no layout state — the client owns that — and keeps prior turns only when you
    configure a history store. It assembles a prompt, calls an LLM, and returns a
    <code>MaelstromResponse</code> with four parts: a <code>userMessage</code>
    (the conversational reply), a <code>rationale</code> (its private reasoning),
    an <code>executionPlan</code> (the actions to run), and a new
    <code>layout</code> (where everything goes).
  </p>

  <h3>The client applies it</h3>
  <p>
    The client applies the response in a fixed order — <strong>layout first, then
    actions</strong>. <code>set_layout</code> positions and sizes every instance;
    <code>execute_actions</code> then runs the <code>executionPlan</code>. The
    order is load-bearing: an action can flip a Vessel to a different variant via
    <code>transitionsTo</code>, and each variant declares its own
    <code>minSize</code>, so positions must be set before actions change them.
  </p>

  <h3>The loop closes</h3>
  <p>
    Applying the response re-renders the UI. The <code>Chart</code>'s
    <code>ResizeObserver</code> measures the result and floors it to whole grid
    cells. If those cells changed, that is a new <code>chart_resize</code> reason —
    and the round-trip runs again. That observer, quietly closing the loop, is what
    lets the layout settle. There is no separate overflow pass; the grid is simply
    clipped with CSS.
  </p>

  <h2>The vocabulary</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt><strong>Vessel</strong></dt>
    <dd>
      A registered component type — an id, a description, and a set of variants.
      The LLM reasons about Vessels by their descriptions. See the
      <a href="/understanding/vessel-state-machine/">vessel state machine</a>.
    </dd>
    <dt><strong>Variant</strong></dt>
    <dd>
      One mode of a Vessel, carrying its props, the actions available in that mode,
      and a <code>minSize</code> — the smallest number of grid cells it needs.
    </dd>
    <dt><strong>Action</strong></dt>
    <dd>
      An operation the engine can invoke on an instance. An action may move the
      instance to another variant via <code>transitionsTo</code>.
    </dd>
    <dt><strong>Chart</strong></dt>
    <dd>
      The surface the Vessels live in. Never pixels: in grid mode the engine
      works in integer cells (<code>rows</code> × <code>columns</code>), and in
      tiling mode — the default — in a preorder tree of <code>H</code>/
      <code>V</code> splits. The <code>ResizeObserver</code> is what converts
      either one to pixels.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <p>
    <strong>The five packages:</strong> <code>protocol</code> is the contract;
    <code>client</code> orchestrates and <code>react</code> binds it to React, with
    the <code>demo</code> app consuming them on the browser side; and
    <code>engine</code> runs the decision on the server.
  </p>

